WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.584 --> 00:00:02.755
- Today we're gonna talk
about how law school

00:00:02.755 --> 00:00:04.832
is just like the LSAT.

00:00:04.832 --> 00:00:08.740
And also how it is completely different.

00:00:08.740 --> 00:00:11.323
(mellow music)

00:00:13.267 --> 00:00:14.779
Lilian reached out to me and said,

00:00:14.779 --> 00:00:17.472
hello, I came across your
page and I'm so glad I did.

00:00:17.472 --> 00:00:19.837
I'm currently preparing to
take the LSAT in September.

00:00:19.837 --> 00:00:22.102
I will hopefully attend
law school next fall.

00:00:22.102 --> 00:00:25.684
I was wondering if you had
any tips on LSAT preparation.

00:00:25.684 --> 00:00:27.271
When I wrote my response to Lilian,

00:00:27.271 --> 00:00:29.734
it actually got me thinking
about how preparing for the LSAT

00:00:29.734 --> 00:00:32.154
was actually kind of similar to the way

00:00:32.154 --> 00:00:34.668
that I prepared for my law school finals.

00:00:34.668 --> 00:00:38.554
So here is a condensed version
of my response to Lilian.

00:00:38.554 --> 00:00:42.098
First tip number one, take
a well-known prep course.

00:00:42.098 --> 00:00:45.297
It's, of course, a lot of
money, but it's money well spent

00:00:45.297 --> 00:00:48.682
because the LSAT is a
test unlike any other test

00:00:48.682 --> 00:00:50.003
you'll ever take.

00:00:50.003 --> 00:00:54.010
It involves reading
comprehension, logic games,

00:00:54.010 --> 00:00:55.815
and it's very unusual.

00:00:55.815 --> 00:00:58.420
But what you find is that
as you become more familiar

00:00:58.420 --> 00:01:02.112
with the LSAT, you learn
the strategies that help,

00:01:02.112 --> 00:01:03.875
you learn how to take logic games,

00:01:03.875 --> 00:01:07.579
you learn how to comprehend
the reading sections

00:01:07.579 --> 00:01:09.057
that are provided.

00:01:09.057 --> 00:01:12.014
It just takes time and it
takes a lot of practice.

00:01:12.014 --> 00:01:14.577
Believe it or not, law school
is actually very similar.

00:01:14.577 --> 00:01:17.693
Law school exams are unlike
anything in undergrad.

00:01:17.693 --> 00:01:19.959
A law school essay is nothing like

00:01:19.959 --> 00:01:22.858
a college exam or college essay.

00:01:22.858 --> 00:01:25.321
But the more you familiarize yourself

00:01:25.321 --> 00:01:29.015
with law school essays, the
better you can become at them.

00:01:29.015 --> 00:01:31.058
Just like there are
strategies for the LSAT,

00:01:31.058 --> 00:01:33.005
there are strategies that you can learn

00:01:33.005 --> 00:01:36.517
to take an issue-spotting
exam and ace it every time.

00:01:36.517 --> 00:01:39.158
It just takes time and it takes practice.

00:01:39.158 --> 00:01:41.623
And that brings me to the
second point I told Lilian.

00:01:41.623 --> 00:01:43.945
Take lots of practice exams.

00:01:43.945 --> 00:01:45.599
Before I took the LSAT,

00:01:45.599 --> 00:01:48.514
I took every single
publicly available LSAT

00:01:48.514 --> 00:01:50.177
that I could get my hands on.

00:01:50.177 --> 00:01:54.092
The summer before my
LSAT, I studied every day,

00:01:54.092 --> 00:01:56.331
probably from nine to five on weekdays

00:01:56.331 --> 00:01:59.053
and probably from nine to six on weekends.

00:01:59.053 --> 00:02:01.116
I considered it my job to take

00:02:01.116 --> 00:02:03.510
as many practice tests as possible.

00:02:03.510 --> 00:02:06.660
Because as I said before, I could see

00:02:06.660 --> 00:02:09.508
that taking the LSAT was a skill.

00:02:09.508 --> 00:02:12.016
And like any skill, you get better at it

00:02:12.016 --> 00:02:13.386
the more you practiced.

00:02:13.386 --> 00:02:14.594
And it's the same with law school.

00:02:14.594 --> 00:02:17.314
Get your hands on as many
practice exams as you can.

00:02:17.314 --> 00:02:20.698
It's okay if you're taking
exams from professors

00:02:20.698 --> 00:02:22.620
that are not at your school.

00:02:22.620 --> 00:02:25.052
It's okay if they're 10 years old.

00:02:25.052 --> 00:02:28.119
In law school, the law that's
taught in your first year

00:02:28.119 --> 00:02:29.785
doesn't really change that much.

00:02:29.785 --> 00:02:33.328
And as a result, you can
take tests from 20 years ago.

00:02:33.328 --> 00:02:36.530
You can take tests from other
schools and other professors.

00:02:36.530 --> 00:02:38.479
Get your hands on as many as you can

00:02:38.479 --> 00:02:40.691
and take as many practice tests as you can

00:02:40.691 --> 00:02:42.243
as early as you can.

00:02:42.243 --> 00:02:45.225
If you do that, you will get
better at law school exams,

00:02:45.225 --> 00:02:47.479
and you are much more likely to get A's

00:02:47.479 --> 00:02:49.922
when it comes to the
actual test in your class.

00:02:49.922 --> 00:02:52.244
And that brought me to the
third thing I told Lilian.

00:02:52.244 --> 00:02:55.979
Take practice exams under
non-ideal circumstances.

00:02:55.979 --> 00:02:58.849
On test day you're going to be stressed,

00:02:58.849 --> 00:03:01.405
you're going to be tired,
you're going to be distracted.

00:03:01.405 --> 00:03:03.026
When Lilian takes the LSAT,

00:03:03.026 --> 00:03:04.354
it's not gonna be on her schedule,

00:03:04.354 --> 00:03:05.710
it's going to be on their schedule.

00:03:05.710 --> 00:03:09.773
It's far too easy to take
practice exams on your schedule,

00:03:09.773 --> 00:03:11.709
after you've had coffee,

00:03:11.709 --> 00:03:13.568
after you've had a good night's sleep,

00:03:13.568 --> 00:03:15.099
when there's no pressure.

00:03:15.099 --> 00:03:17.552
Once you've established
a baseline competence,

00:03:17.552 --> 00:03:19.783
take practice tests with the music on,

00:03:19.783 --> 00:03:22.821
with other distractions,
immediately upon waking up,

00:03:22.821 --> 00:03:24.354
or at three o'clock in the morning.

00:03:24.354 --> 00:03:28.340
And always take practice exams
under real time pressure,

00:03:28.340 --> 00:03:30.596
because I guarantee when you walk in

00:03:30.596 --> 00:03:32.968
to your first law school final exam,

00:03:32.968 --> 00:03:35.020
you're going to be freaked out.

00:03:35.020 --> 00:03:36.442
It's a natural reaction.

00:03:36.442 --> 00:03:40.113
But if you can practice under
non-ideal circumstances,

00:03:40.113 --> 00:03:41.983
then you're going to be way ahead

00:03:41.983 --> 00:03:43.387
of all of those other people

00:03:43.387 --> 00:03:44.871
who are taking the same exam

00:03:44.871 --> 00:03:47.406
and are even more
freaked out than you are.

00:03:47.406 --> 00:03:49.224
When I responded to
Lilian, I realized that

00:03:49.224 --> 00:03:52.694
taking the LSAT and taking
a law school final exam

00:03:52.694 --> 00:03:54.482
and the way that you prepare for them

00:03:54.482 --> 00:03:55.700
are actually pretty similar.

00:03:55.700 --> 00:03:58.503
Unfortunately, there is
little to no carry over

00:03:58.503 --> 00:04:00.883
between the strategies
that go into the LSAT

00:04:00.883 --> 00:04:03.928
and the strategies that go
into a law school final exam.

00:04:03.928 --> 00:04:06.518
Issue spotting exams
are just two different.

00:04:06.518 --> 00:04:09.400
But the underlying
methodology is the same.

00:04:09.400 --> 00:04:11.142
So when you get to law school,

00:04:11.142 --> 00:04:13.228
don't throw the baby
out with the bath water.

00:04:13.228 --> 00:04:15.397
Use your good habits that you learned

00:04:15.397 --> 00:04:18.597
when you practiced the LSAT
and bring that into law school,

00:04:18.597 --> 00:04:21.764
and you'll be way ahead of everybody else.

00:04:21.764 --> 00:04:23.596
Oh, and one last thing.

